WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.700
Here is a cell on your screen.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.783
We have already seen that the various organelles

00:00:09.783 --> 00:00:12.480
inside it are compactly packed inside this

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.151
delicate membrane called the cell membrane

00:00:15.151 --> 00:00:16.360
or plasma membrane!

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.712
We have even seen that the cell membrane makes

00:00:19.712 --> 00:00:22.700
use of diffusion process to transport gas

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.000
and water molecules in and out of the cell.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.350
However, Diffusion is not the only process employed.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:31.900
There are a few more in the list.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.450
Let us have a look at these methods in this video.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.435
Tell me one thing, how do we transport water

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.080
from a lower height to a higher height?

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:43.800
We need a pump.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.160
Because this does not happen on its own.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.080
A force or some energy is required to pull

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.040
the water upwards.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.505
Similarly in a cell, some molecules have to

00:00:55.505 --> 00:00:59.040
be carried inside or moved outside the cell.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.600
This requires energy to be spent.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.670
The energy used is the form of ATP molecules.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.630
This is called as ACTIVE Transport.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.910
That is, ACTIVE transport requires expenditure

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:14.299
of energy.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.940
The transportation is carried out by protein

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.759
molecules present on the cell membrane.

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:23.680
On the other hand, diffusion, does not require energy.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.270
Hence it is a type of PASSIVE TRANSPORT.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.720
So movement of molecules without spending

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.320
energy as in case of diffusion is passive

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.420
transport.

00:01:33.749 --> 00:01:36.500
Similarly, movement of molecules across the

00:01:36.515 --> 00:01:39.409
membrane with the expense of energy is active

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:40.400
transport!

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.400
Now let us get to know one more interesting

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.660
concept named OSMOSIS.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:48.189
Have we ever come across this term OSMOSIS?

00:01:48.189 --> 00:01:50.799
Well, quite often!

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:54.039
But what exactly do we mean by this?

00:01:54.039 --> 00:01:56.406
Osmosis is defined as the movement of water

00:01:56.406 --> 00:01:58.939
from higher water concentration to lower water

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:02.979
concentration through a semi permeable membrane.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:05.584
So let us say we have this container which

00:02:05.584 --> 00:02:08.500
is partitioned by this semi permeable membrane.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:12.040
Now we add sugar solutions at both these sides.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.570
However, the concentration of each is different.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.980
Here we add concentrated sugar solution while

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.720
here, dilute sugar solution.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.460
That means the concentration of water in this

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:28.020
solution is obviously more compared to this!

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.449
So after some time, we see water molecules

00:02:30.449 --> 00:02:32.820
moving from this side of the partition to

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.410
this side.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.450
And how long will this movement take place?

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.238
It will occur till the concentration of water

00:02:40.238 --> 00:02:42.840
is balanced, that is equalized on both the

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.500
sides!

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.440
This is nothing but osmosis!

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.300
Let us learn some more interesting concepts

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.720
about osmosis and cell transport in the next

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.530
video!

